# The catalogue of every lint code the plan engine emits: one entry per F-code.
# Read by `phase-graph.sh --explain <code>` and by the lint list in `--help`.
# `references/plan-format.md` quotes it. NEVER sourced by bash: an awk reads it, so
# write nothing here that needs a shell.
#
# Format. A record line at column 0: `<CODE> <name> <tier>`; the name is the kebab id
# the lint prints (several ids for one code are joined with `,`, no spaces); the tier
# is `gate` (fails --lint / validate.sh) or `advisory` (stderr only, exit untouched).
# Then the paragraph, every line indented by exactly two spaces, then a blank line.
# Entries run in numeric order. A `# !pro:start` ... `# !pro:end` region is Pro only.

F1 phase-cell-malformed gate
  A cell in the Phase graph table's Phase column holds a digit but is not a bare integer ("2a", "6
  (gated)"), so the parser silently drops that row. The lint also fails when the frontmatter
  `phases:` count disagrees with the number of rows the table parses. Write each phase number as a
  plain integer in the first column, and keep `phases:` equal to the row count.

F2 dependency-undefined gate
  A phase's Depends-on cell names a phase number that is not in the table. The dependency can never
  clear, so the phase would wait on nothing real. Name only phases the table has, or add the missing
  row.

F3 dependency-cycle gate
  The dependency graph has a cycle (the lint prints the path, for example `2 -> 3 -> 2`). Phases in
  a cycle can never become ready. Break the loop by removing one Depends-on edge.

F10 handoff-invalid gate
  `validate.sh` found a handoff that cannot be trusted. Its `status:` is not one of complete,
  in-progress, blocked or pending, or it lacks the "Start next phase(s)" boot section, or its
  `depends_on:` disagrees with the plan graph. A handoff must be actionable and agree with the plan,
  because the board and the next session both read it.

F14 verification-empty-open gate
  An open, not-done phase has no runnable command in its section "Verification". The autopilot would
  park the phase at boarding after it was paid for, and a prose-only verification card once asked a
  person for twelve hours. Add backticked commands or a fenced block that prove the exit criteria;
  backticked numbers alone do not count. A done phase is not judged, and a closed plan is noted, not
  gated.

F15 registry-name-unknown advisory
  The plan or a phase names an MCP server, a credential or an account that this machine's console
  has not registered. The autopilot would park the phase at boarding, or run it without the server
  and report it, depending on the resolved policy. Register the name in the console, or drop it from
  the plan. The check is off when the console has told the engine nothing about its registries.

F16 verification-unbounded advisory
  A Verification command waits on an external clock: `gh run watch`, a deploy that needs a CI-built
  image, a `--watch` or `wait` flag, a long `sleep`, or an `until`/`while` poll loop. The runner
  bounds each command (30 minutes by default) and an unattended session cannot outlive its turn.
  Split the phase into a build phase and a verify-later phase behind a Gate-check, or expect a
  runtime park as `waiting`.

F17 verification-lead-missing advisory
  The lead binary of a Verification command is not installed on this machine, for example `rg` or
  `python`. The autopilot skips such a command at verification (recorded, not failed), and a phase
  whose every check is skipped parks. Write the check with what exists: `grep -R` for `rg`,
  `python3` for `python`.

F18 verification-cwd-unset advisory
  A cwd-sensitive lead (`pnpm`, `npm`, `docker`, `task`, `pytest`) appears in Verification while the
  phase has no `- **Verify in:** <dir>`. Verification runs at the repository root, where the command
  may test the wrong tree and report a spurious failure. Add `Verify in:` for the directory, or
  prefix the command with `cd <dir> &&`.

F19 plan-cannot-progress advisory
  The whole plan cannot move: nothing is ready, nothing is in flight, and a QA verdict holds every
  remaining phase. It fires once for the board, naming the rows responsible, not once per phase.
  Re-run QA to pass or waived with `scripts/qa-record.sh`, set `**QA gate:** off` in the Session
  budget, or close the plan.

F20 table-shape-invalid gate
  The Phase graph table is not the table the parser thinks it is: no header row, no `Depends on`
  column, no `Repos` column, or a row shorter than the header. Every scope and readiness answer is
  read from those columns, so without them every phase looks free of dependencies or scoped to
  `all`, and two sessions can end up in one working tree. Use the header `| Phase | Title | Depends
  on | Parallel-safe with | Repos | Exit criteria |` and fill every row.

F21 table-cell-unbelievable,phase-section-repeated,handoff-scaffold-complete gate
  The engine read something it could not fully believe: a Depends-on token it discarded, a Phase row
  repeated in the table, a zero-padded number, two `### Phase N` headings for one phase, a phase
  with two handoff files, or a handoff that says complete while its "What this phase did" is still
  the template. Each used to be silent, and the board then answered about a different plan than the
  one on disk. Write plain numbers, one row, one section and one handoff per phase.

F22 verification-bring-up advisory
  A Verification line is bring-up rather than proof: `docker compose up -d`, `npm ci`, a `sleep`
  after them. Inside Verification it is a command the boarding preflight asks a person to vouch
  for, and it can turn a phase red for a reason unrelated to its work. Move it to `- **Setup:**`,
  which runs before Verification and never marks the phase red.

F23 verification-prose-expectation advisory
  A Verification line states an expected failure in prose beside a command, such as "expected to
  fail until Phase 9". The runner executes the command and takes its exit code; the sentence is
  invisible to it, so the phase goes red. Encode the expectation in the command itself (`! <cmd>`,
  or a grep for the specific error).

F24 gate-directive-missing,gate-type-unknown gate
  A `*(GATED)*` heading has no `- **Gate-check:** <type> <value>` bullet, or the bullet's type is
  not on the list in `scripts/gates.env`. The heading then reads as the default type (`ai`), and a
  typo used to demote an automated gate to a person silently. The default answers the board, but the
  lint makes the author say it. Write the directive with a known type.

F25 decision-outstanding-unowned,decision-key-unknown,decision-state-unknown,decision-source-unknown gate
  A row of the plan's decisions manifest is unusable: an `outstanding` row with no owner, a key
  outside the decision vocabulary, a state other than answered, outstanding or waived, or a source
  outside the four allowed. Nobody can answer an unowned decision, and the readers drop rows they
  do not know. Name the owner (who answers it) and use only the vocabulary of `decisions.env`.

F26 note-target-unknown gate
  A handoff's "Notes for later phases" bullet is addressed to a phase the plan does not have.
  Nothing will ever deliver it. Re-address it to a phase number in the plan.

F27 land-word-unknown gate
  A closed directive carries a word outside its list, such as `Land: sometimes` or `Issues: maybe`,
  on a plan-wide line (Landing, Gitlink, Conflicts, Messaging, Issues, Isolation) or on a phase
  bullet. Readers fall through an unknown word to the next level, so a typo would behave exactly as
  if the plan had never said anything. The lint names the directive, the level and the word; use one
  of the listed words.

F28 land-needs-lane advisory
  A phase lands (anything but `hold`) from a checkout it shares with others, so the merge carries
  every commit on that branch and not just its own. Give the phase `- **Isolation:** worktree`, or
  land it `hold` and let one phase land for all of them. It is a hazard rather than an error: a
  serial plan whose phases all land the same way is fine.

F29 landed-gate-unknown-phase gate
  A `landed` or `pr-merged` Gate-check names no valid phase: the value is not a number, it names the
  gated phase itself, or it names a phase the plan does not have. Such a gate can never clear, and
  reads exactly like a phase that has not landed yet. Name one phase number that is in the plan.

F30 note-target-done advisory
  A handoff note is addressed to a phase that is already done, so nothing will ever board with it.
  This is routinely true and harmless: a phase re-run out of order, or a note written to a sibling
  that finished first. Re-address the note, or let it stand as a record of what was said. It has its
  own code because an id that both gates and warns cannot answer "did the lint fail?".

F31 permission-mode-unknown gate
  A `Permission mode:` line or bullet names a word that is not one of `scripts/permission.env`'s
  (`acceptEdits`, `auto`, `dontAsk`, `plan`, `manual`). Readers fall through an unknown word, so
  `paln` would run the phase in whatever the run defaults to. `bypassPermissions` is not a mode; it
  is the run's profile. Spell one of the listed modes.

F32 verification-fleet-wide advisory
  A Verification line's exit code reads fleet-wide state, such as `task hygiene`, `task drift` or a
  machine-wide `ps ... | grep`, instead of what this plan did. In a shared checkout it cannot pass
  while any other plan, branch or console is live. Assert the plan's own footprint instead (for
  example `test -z "$(git -C <repo> status --porcelain)"`), and leave the fleet audit to a person
  behind a `Gate-check: manual`.

F33 repos-outside-root advisory
  A Repos token is not a path under the docs root, in a plan whose other cells are. That phase alone
  is refused isolation: it runs in the run's checkout with an unqualified lock claim and serialises
  against every claim it intersects. Name only paths this root contains, and say in the phase's prose
  which outside repository it works in.

F34 repos-root-token advisory
  A Repos cell names the superproject's own root, but the phase's `Files` bullet lists only paths in
  submodules or under `docs/`. The token mounts the whole root with every submodule and holds the
  phase behind any run on the root repository, for nothing. List the submodules the phase writes
  instead; its handoff, INDEX and lock writes are declared scope already.

F35 checkout-inert advisory
  A phase's `Checkout:` bullet names a value other than `main`, `master` or `default`. The console
  carries such a value verbatim and acts on it never, so a session expected on that branch runs on
  the run branch and nobody is told. Write `default`, or drop the bullet and name the branch in the
  phase's prose.

F36 wait-window-short advisory
  A `Waits on:` maximum is shorter than the timeout of the workflow it watches, so the wait can
  never outlast the job. The console tells the engine the timeouts; with none told, the check is
  off. Raise the maximum to at least the workflow's timeout, with `wait-budget.sh` or on the phase.

F37 human-step-superseded,human-step-kind-unknown,human-step-field-invalid gate
  A `Human step:` bullet that the reader would skip: the 5.1.0 `<who, what, proof ref>` spelling,
  which nothing ever parsed; a kind that is not one of the allowed kinds; or a field the grammar
  does not have (a `due:` that fits no scheme, a `why:` the kind does not allow). The lint gates
  because a skipped step is a person's act nobody is told about. The message gives the replacement
  grammar.

F38 human-step-no-proof advisory
  A `Human step:` bullet names no `proof:` ref, so only a person's word can close it and nothing can
  check it. Add `proof: <ref>` in one of the forms the console's check reads.

F39 setup-deps-missing advisory
  A Verification line runs a package-manager script or a `.venv/bin/*` binary in a repository that
  the phase's Setup never installs. A run's isolated checkout mounts every repository fresh, with no
  `node_modules` or `.venv`, so the line fails before it tests anything (#185). Add the install to
  `- **Setup:**`, spelled from the Verify-in directory, because Setup runs where Verification runs.

F40 human-step-no-why advisory
  A `Human step:` bullet names no `why:`. The item is given its kind's default reason, marked
  inferred, so it is never a gate. Add `why: <reason>` saying why only a person fits the act.

F41 human-step-no-guide advisory
  A task or decision step whose words are the plan's own (an operator-act, a physical act, a
  third-party-approval, a decision) has no `guide:`, no `options:` and no bound `## Operator
  errands` row. The person would get a title and no steps. Add `guide: <file>`, or a table row whose
  text ends with `(E-N)`. Kinds the console walks a person through itself are not judged.

F42 guide-file-missing gate
  A `Human step:` bullet's `guide:` names a path that is not a file under the docs root. The console
  reads that file at the launch door and on every read of Your turn, and a missing one costs the
  item its guide. Point `guide:` at an existing file inside the docs root.

F43 errand-row-unbound advisory
  A row of the plan's `## Operator errands` table has no `Human step:` bullet whose text ends with
  its id, such as `(E-3)`. Nothing checks the row or waits on it, so it is shown as a heads-up only.
  Bind it by ending a bullet's text with the row's id, or leave it as a note on purpose.


F45 proof-unparsable advisory
  A `Human step:` proof is one the console's check would refuse every time: a `cmd:` ref with a
  shell variable, a substitution, an unclosed quote or a path relative to a session's cwd, or a
  proof with no watch scheme. Rewrite it with literal, absolute paths and a known scheme. The
  engine reads it through `scripts/cmd-ref.sh`, the bash twin of the console's one `cmd:` reader.

F46 proof-script-run-branch-only advisory
  A `cmd:` proof runs a script that exists only on the run's branch, not on its repository's trunk.
  The console checks a proof from its own checkout, so until the script lands there the check exits
  127 and reads unrunnable. Land the script first, or prove the step another way.

F47 skill-unresolved advisory
  A skill the plan's `**Skills (every session):**` line or a phase's `- **Skills:**` bullet names is
  not among the skills this console's unattended sessions load (`skill-unresolved`), or is marked
  `disable-model-invocation`, which the Skill tool refuses (`skill-user-invoked`). The console tells
  the engine its set through `PE_SKILLS`, the way F15 is told `PE_MCP_SERVERS`; by hand it is unset
  and the lint is silent. Seed the plugin or switch it on for autopilot, or drop the name.

F48 plugin-set-over-budget advisory
  A phase's plugin set — the entries its `- **Plugins:**` kinds bring, unioned with the plan's
  `**Plugins:**` line (`scripts/plugin-sets.env`) — would list more than the CLI's skill-listing
  budget allows: each entry costed at its 1,536-character ceiling, against 1 % of the phase
  model's window at 4 characters a token (8,000 characters, five entries, on 200 K; 1 M with
  `[1m]`). Name fewer kinds or split the phase. The same code names a word on a `Plugins:` line
  that is no kind (`plugin-kind-unknown`), which brings nothing. Sets are never unioned across phases.

F49 amendment-malformed advisory
  The two halves of a plan amendment disagree. A phase carries `- **Amendment:** K` but the preamble
  under the plan's title has no `> *(Amendment K, YYYY-MM-DD)*` note; or a note names a phase the
  plan does not have, or a phase whose section says another number; or a note names no phase and no
  phase carries its number; or a line opens `*(Amendment` and is not that shape. `scripts/amend-plan.sh`
  writes the section, its graph row and the note in that order and is the one writer: use it, or write
  both halves by hand.

F50 directive-mid-line advisory
  A known directive label (a Session budget label such as `Verify timeout:`, `Wait count:` or
  `Issues:`, or a phase bullet label such as `Model:`) is found mid-line, where no reader reads it.
  Since measured-console phase 2 a Session budget line may carry several directives separated by
  ` · `, and every plan-wide reader reads each; a label after a comma or prose, or a second label
  inside a phase bullet, is still read by nobody (#419). Give the label its own line, or its own
  `- **Label:**` bullet in a phase.

F51 path-no-dir advisory
  A Repos token or a `Verify in:` path names no directory under the docs root, and is not `all`, the
  root's own token or a submodule path. A run fails to mount it at boarding, and the lint used to
  pass it (#402). Spell the path from the docs root as it is on disk: `admin`, not `web/admin`,
  when the repository sits at `admin`.

F52 verification-shared-checkout advisory
  A Verification or Setup line has a `cd`, `git -C`, `--prefix` or `-d`/`--dir` target that is an
  absolute path under the docs root: the shared checkout, which is on `main`. In an isolated run the
  line then tests main, not the run's branch (#423). Write the path relative to the phase's
  `Verify in:` directory: `cd ../api` from `apps/web`, `cd apps/api`
  from `.`.
